WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:08.550
MACIEK ROZACKI: Hi, welcome
to designing Google Kubernetes

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:10.860
Clusters for Massive
Scale and Performance

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:15.300
session, where we will talk
how Google and Twitter joined

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:20.670
forces to push the limits
of what GKE and Kubernetes

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:22.200
technologies can do.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:24.750
And we'll also give you
a set of best practices

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:27.570
how to think about
running at large scale

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:31.250
with good reliability
and performance.

00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:33.060
I'm Maciek Rozacki.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:36.030
And I am a product
manager at Google.

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:38.190
REZA MOTAMEDI: Hi,
and I'm Reza Motamedi.

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:40.350
And I'm a software
engineer at Twitter.

00:00:42.802 --> 00:00:44.260
MACIEK ROZACKI:
First off, as we're

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:46.990
going to talk about
scalability, let's

00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:50.290
nail down the definition
of what it actually means.

00:00:50.290 --> 00:00:54.530
If you scan the internet
for explanations

00:00:54.530 --> 00:00:58.280
what scalability is, you'll
find numerous definitions.

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:04.750
But they all boil down to a
characteristic or capability

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:09.550
of a system to work with very
significant amount of resources

00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:12.890
while still being able to
operate with reliability,

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:16.780
performance, and, essentially,
do the job that the system was

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:18.280
meant to do.

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:22.160
When it comes to Kubernetes, we
expect that in most use cases,

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:24.490
you will want to run on
more than one cluster,

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:28.450
to minimize the risk of any
potential [INAUDIBLE] problems

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:31.960
with any outages, still
being able to operate,

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:37.300
also, large clusters
will simplify many parts

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:39.340
of how you're managing
your software,

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:43.630
starting from easier ability
to manage the microservices

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:45.580
that you're running,
for example, by avoiding

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:51.160
the need for excessive sharding
to reduced operational toil

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:54.040
on managing a fleet of clusters.

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:57.670
There are also scenarios
where simply running

00:01:57.670 --> 00:01:59.590
on a single, very
large cluster makes

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:03.070
the most sense, for example, for
highly parallel-izeable batch

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:04.480
jobs.

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:10.030
And last but not least, it's
very useful, in many cases,

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:13.630
to be able to operate in
the lower bound of what

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:16.780
a particular technology can
do, like Kubernetes, and have

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:19.630
a buffer to the
scale limits of it.

00:02:19.630 --> 00:02:22.210
So that you can
absorb large spikes.

00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:24.760
For example, when you're
running an e-commerce store,

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:27.640
and from time to time, you
have very significant spikes

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:31.780
coming from sales or
events like Black Friday.

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:36.590
When you think about companies
that run at massive scale

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:39.490
and operate huge
infrastructure, Twitter

00:02:39.490 --> 00:02:41.840
is going to be one of
the largest in the world.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:45.220
So let's listen from Reza
how they are maintaining

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:46.600
their infrastructure
and how they

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:50.030
think about its
evolution in the future.

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:51.490
REZA MOTAMEDI: Thanks, Maciek.

00:02:51.490 --> 00:02:54.490
I'm going to briefly talk about
the compute infrastructure

00:02:54.490 --> 00:02:58.270
Twitter, where we are today,
and where we're going,

00:02:58.270 --> 00:03:02.860
and how Kubernetes, in
general, and GKE, specifically,

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:06.070
fits into our plans.

00:03:06.070 --> 00:03:08.290
The current main compute
offering at Twitter

00:03:08.290 --> 00:03:12.550
is powered by Mesos Cluster
Manager and Aurora Scheduler.

00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:16.910
Twitter embraced containers,
starting in 2012.

00:03:16.910 --> 00:03:20.570
We used super lightweight
containers with Linux cgroups.

00:03:20.570 --> 00:03:25.640
We're not using virtual machine
or Docker level containers.

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:30.420
But we cgroups, we are able to
collocate most of the workloads

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:32.960
on compute infrastructure.

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:35.840
Currently, our compute
offering is mostly on-premise.

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:39.860
But we are extending it
with computing cloud.

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:42.140
Our customers, who
are Twitter engineers,

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:46.880
hire us to manage the lifecycle
of their micro services that,

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:50.810
as a whole, power the Twitter
conversation platform.

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:55.580
There are different categories
of these microservices.

00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:57.800
First, there are
stateless applications.

00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:01.400
These are mostly fault-tolerant
real-time services that

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:04.130
are very sensitive to latency.

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:07.820
There's also a sizable amount
of offline batch workload,

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:11.540
mostly doing machine learning,
which is also stateless.

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:14.420
Second, we have services
that are kind of stateful,

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:17.120
meaning they keep
some sort of state.

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:19.940
But the state is not
generally durable.

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:22.880
These includes caches,
and search indices,

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:25.190
and other services like that.

00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:28.100
These services are not
so forgiving in the face

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:28.860
of failure.

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:32.600
So for them, both performance
and platform reliability

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:35.150
is important.

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:38.680
It is also important to note
that compute infrastructure was

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:41.680
built from the ground
up to be multi-tenant.

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:45.500
So most microservices live
in the same shared pool.

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:47.950
We generally like
multi-tenancy, because it

00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:52.120
scales in multiple directions,
from resource efficiency

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:54.550
to operational efficiency,
and also helps us

00:04:54.550 --> 00:04:56.470
with capacity management.

00:04:56.470 --> 00:04:59.560
The key win for
compute team has been

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:02.320
that all these
different services use

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:05.680
the same configuration,
deployment, debugging,

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:07.990
and management interfaces.

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:09.850
This allows us to
build the tooling

00:05:09.850 --> 00:05:13.540
on top of a single platform
that benefits all engineering

00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:16.720
teams at Twitter.

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:19.450
In terms of scale, we
run a smaller number

00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:21.580
of large Mesos clusters.

00:05:21.580 --> 00:05:26.500
Each compute cluster has in the
order of millions of CPU cores,

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:30.520
hundreds of thousands of
containers, tens of thousands

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:32.740
of hosts, and
thousands of services

00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:34.990
are running on each cluster.

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:38.950
Our microservices have also
adapted to our large clusters,

00:05:38.950 --> 00:05:42.160
meaning that we host more
than a few services that each

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:45.100
have thousands of instances.

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:47.860
In terms of operation,
we manage and maintain

00:05:47.860 --> 00:05:52.450
all of our clusters with only 10
to 15 software and reliability

00:05:52.450 --> 00:05:54.670
engineers

00:05:54.670 --> 00:05:58.090
With that intro, let's see where
Twitter wants to go from here

00:05:58.090 --> 00:06:03.220
and how that affects our
plans for the compute team.

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:05.950
There is-- Twitter's
main mission

00:06:05.950 --> 00:06:09.790
is to serve the public,
global conversation.

00:06:09.790 --> 00:06:14.080
For this, we need to complement
our own on-premise compute

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:19.010
infrastructure with offerings
from public cloud providers.

00:06:19.010 --> 00:06:22.120
We already leverage public
cloud for some workloads,

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:25.300
for example, those that benefit
from global distribution.

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:29.180
And we expect this trend
to increase over time.

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:32.110
But as we explore
using the cloud,

00:06:32.110 --> 00:06:33.760
we don't want our
customers to deal

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:36.550
with fragmented
compute interfaces,

00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:40.160
both in on-premise
and in the cloud.

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:44.470
So our goal is to keep providing
a unified experience to Twitter

00:06:44.470 --> 00:06:45.850
engineers.

00:06:45.850 --> 00:06:50.470
Also, as we run compute platform
in more environment that

00:06:50.470 --> 00:06:55.630
offer new primitives, we
see new opportunities.

00:06:55.630 --> 00:07:00.100
We can extend this standard
microservice management layer

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:03.040
that we have today
across an even more

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:05.960
diverse set of services.

00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:09.160
This means that we can
onboard the remaining services

00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:13.600
onto our platform, for example,
stateful services that are

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:17.030
running on bare metal today.

00:07:17.030 --> 00:07:20.330
This will, basically, improve
developers' experience

00:07:20.330 --> 00:07:22.400
and productivity
and will eventually

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:26.990
reduce the run the business
costs for everyone at Twitter.

00:07:26.990 --> 00:07:29.810
To deal with the potential
additional complexity,

00:07:29.810 --> 00:07:33.860
the compute team did a detailed
evaluation and selected

00:07:33.860 --> 00:07:35.720
Kubernetes as the
technology that

00:07:35.720 --> 00:07:39.230
would power the next generation
Twitter compute platform,

00:07:39.230 --> 00:07:42.221
both in cloud and on-premise.

00:07:42.221 --> 00:07:45.350
This simplifies our
job, since compute team

00:07:45.350 --> 00:07:49.850
needs to be expert and
manage a single technology,

00:07:49.850 --> 00:07:52.980
and provides a unified
experience to our customers,

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:56.550
regardless of where their
services are running.

00:07:56.550 --> 00:08:02.240
It also gives us the benefit
and support of a vibrant open

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:04.020
source community.

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:07.880
But all of this means
that Kubernetes and GKE

00:08:07.880 --> 00:08:11.180
have to scale to Twitter
needs, at least to some extent,

00:08:11.180 --> 00:08:15.060
for an acceptable
migration path.

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:18.210
MACIEK ROZACKI: So having heard
the requirements and the needs

00:08:18.210 --> 00:08:22.800
of Twitter, we set off to
push the limits of what

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:27.390
our technology
can offer and meet

00:08:27.390 --> 00:08:31.410
the requirements of Twitter.

00:08:31.410 --> 00:08:35.640
Now, as we set off to
document those requirements,

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:39.630
first, I would like to explain
to you the framework that we

00:08:39.630 --> 00:08:46.590
use to document the requirements
and scalability of Kubernetes

00:08:46.590 --> 00:08:47.580
in general.

00:08:47.580 --> 00:08:49.900
It's a framework that
we use in this context.

00:08:49.900 --> 00:08:53.190
But we also recommend
that you leverage it

00:08:53.190 --> 00:08:56.130
as you also think about
your architecture,

00:08:56.130 --> 00:08:58.710
whether in the initial phases,
or as you're diagnosing

00:08:58.710 --> 00:09:01.260
an existing and growing system.

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:05.730
Very frequently, when we
talk about Kubernetes scale,

00:09:05.730 --> 00:09:09.450
we use the number of nodes
as a proxy to describe,

00:09:09.450 --> 00:09:13.620
in a simplified fashion,
the scale of the cluster.

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:16.740
As your workloads
grow in complexity,

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:18.510
this becomes insufficient.

00:09:18.510 --> 00:09:22.410
You may have scenarios where
you don't use too many nodes,

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:24.280
but still hit
scalability problems.

00:09:24.280 --> 00:09:29.140
For example, if you excessively
use services in your cluster.

00:09:29.140 --> 00:09:33.900
So a better way to think about
your clusters and workloads

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:36.750
is through a
multidimensional hypercube,

00:09:36.750 --> 00:09:38.520
where, on each of
the dimensions,

00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:41.310
you describe the resources
that your clusters

00:09:41.310 --> 00:09:42.980
and workloads need.

00:09:42.980 --> 00:09:46.500
And on all of those
dimensions, there

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:48.780
would be an envelope
that wraps all

00:09:48.780 --> 00:09:50.760
of the acceptable
configurations.

00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:52.890
Where if you don't break
those limits, and don't

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:54.840
go beyond that
envelope, you will

00:09:54.840 --> 00:09:59.790
be able to say that
your cluster scales.

00:09:59.790 --> 00:10:02.620
So it can use the
resources that you need.

00:10:02.620 --> 00:10:07.240
And it gives a healthy
service to your users.

00:10:07.240 --> 00:10:09.630
The dimensions that you
can see here on the screen

00:10:09.630 --> 00:10:10.630
are just examples.

00:10:10.630 --> 00:10:13.110
There is many, many more
that you should account for.

00:10:13.110 --> 00:10:14.850
You can find them
in our documentation

00:10:14.850 --> 00:10:16.710
of various materials
on the topic.

00:10:16.710 --> 00:10:19.050
Here, we want to, essentially,
explain the concept,

00:10:19.050 --> 00:10:20.760
how to think about this.

00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:22.320
Also, there might
be dimensions that

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:25.350
will be very unique and specific
to your particular workload

00:10:25.350 --> 00:10:27.180
with the abstraction
layers and extensions

00:10:27.180 --> 00:10:29.770
that you will add
to your cluster.

00:10:29.770 --> 00:10:31.830
I would like to
draw your attention

00:10:31.830 --> 00:10:35.840
to a couple of characteristics
of the scalability envelope

00:10:35.840 --> 00:10:38.700
for Kubernetes that are very
important when you think

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:42.660
about scaling your workloads.

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:45.600
First of all, the
dimensions of that cube

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:47.520
depend on one another.

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:50.700
And moreover, the functions
that describe those dependencies

00:10:50.700 --> 00:10:53.610
are not linear and frequently
are somewhat convex.

00:10:53.610 --> 00:10:55.530
For example, you
can, theoretically,

00:10:55.530 --> 00:10:58.030
run 110 Pods on every node.

00:10:58.030 --> 00:10:59.970
But if you grow your
cluster to match, then

00:10:59.970 --> 00:11:03.900
with additional nodes, the
acceptable port density

00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:05.710
will be very good going down.

00:11:05.710 --> 00:11:08.430
And with large
clusters, you will not

00:11:08.430 --> 00:11:11.610
be able to go above
30 Pods by node.

00:11:11.610 --> 00:11:15.210
The envelope tapers
along each axis, which

00:11:15.210 --> 00:11:18.630
means that if you excessively
use a particular resource

00:11:18.630 --> 00:11:20.880
on your cluster, then you
will get much less freedom

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:26.070
on all of the other dimensions
with your workloads.

00:11:26.070 --> 00:11:28.650
The whole envelope
is bounded, primarily

00:11:28.650 --> 00:11:31.770
by the limits of how much
data it can store on etcd.

00:11:31.770 --> 00:11:35.220
So there are points that
you cannot just break wit

00:11:35.220 --> 00:11:38.550
by stretching the envelope
in just one dimension.

00:11:38.550 --> 00:11:41.820
But what's pretty positive about
the behavior of this envelope

00:11:41.820 --> 00:11:44.080
is that it's decomposable
into smaller ones.

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:46.860
So there are subsets of
dimensions with which you

00:11:46.860 --> 00:11:50.940
can play, independently from
other subsets, and this way,

00:11:50.940 --> 00:11:55.180
take a simplified approach to
analyzing your architecture.

00:11:55.180 --> 00:11:57.270
So being equipped
with this framework

00:11:57.270 --> 00:12:00.450
of describing
requirements, we documented

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:01.350
the needs of Twitter.

00:12:01.350 --> 00:12:03.870
We also engaged a few
other design partners.

00:12:03.870 --> 00:12:08.190
And we worked with a broader
group of customers and users

00:12:08.190 --> 00:12:11.130
who we know could
benefit from operating

00:12:11.130 --> 00:12:15.060
at larger scale than currently
supported in Kubernetes.

00:12:15.060 --> 00:12:19.020
And based on that
analysis, we came up

00:12:19.020 --> 00:12:22.530
with a set of generic
solutions, a portfolio

00:12:22.530 --> 00:12:28.170
of use cases, in which
we believe users really

00:12:28.170 --> 00:12:30.120
need very large scale.

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:35.460
And we have an understanding
of how the various dependencies

00:12:35.460 --> 00:12:39.540
between dimensions need to
be pushed, so that we offer

00:12:39.540 --> 00:12:41.465
a very good end-to-end
experience for all

00:12:41.465 --> 00:12:44.940
of the workloads that users will
be running on those clusters.

00:12:44.940 --> 00:12:48.120
That equipped us to
set a set of targets,

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:49.920
where we want to
push the existing

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:53.610
limits of the technology
forward to meet

00:12:53.610 --> 00:12:57.750
the needs of more and
more demanding users.

00:12:57.750 --> 00:13:03.570
The primary dimension that we
decided to improve and move

00:13:03.570 --> 00:13:06.250
is the number of
nodes up to 15,000,

00:13:06.250 --> 00:13:09.570
so three times bigger than
the currently supported limit.

00:13:09.570 --> 00:13:12.880
In terms of the number of Pods,
it's half a million by cluster.

00:13:12.880 --> 00:13:16.260
We also invested
heavily into improving

00:13:16.260 --> 00:13:18.570
the various characteristics
of networking components

00:13:18.570 --> 00:13:22.200
in Kubernetes like using
very large services,

00:13:22.200 --> 00:13:28.650
and many, many other aspects
of how we run clusters.

00:13:28.650 --> 00:13:31.680
You can look for details
in our documentation, where

00:13:31.680 --> 00:13:33.780
we publish more
specific information

00:13:33.780 --> 00:13:36.390
about each dimension.

00:13:36.390 --> 00:13:40.020
To effectively work on pushing
the limits of the technology,

00:13:40.020 --> 00:13:45.300
we augmented and extended
our testing framework

00:13:45.300 --> 00:13:48.810
through additional
SLIs and SLOs,

00:13:48.810 --> 00:13:52.140
extending the test suite with
additional benchmark workloads,

00:13:52.140 --> 00:13:56.590
both synthetic and emulating
real customer scenarios.

00:13:56.590 --> 00:13:58.920
So that, along the way,
as we are progressing

00:13:58.920 --> 00:14:01.350
towards pushing the
limits of the technology,

00:14:01.350 --> 00:14:03.960
we are able to
effectively diagnose

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:06.270
where we are on that
journey, and are we already

00:14:06.270 --> 00:14:11.190
able to offer highly
reliable and scalable service

00:14:11.190 --> 00:14:12.930
to our customers.

00:14:12.930 --> 00:14:17.130
The improvements that we
implemented on the journey

00:14:17.130 --> 00:14:19.020
come in a few areas.

00:14:19.020 --> 00:14:21.780
The main one, which is
probably also the most obvious,

00:14:21.780 --> 00:14:26.550
is the fact that the Kubernetes
open source software required

00:14:26.550 --> 00:14:30.150
a lot of tuning and improvements
that we implemented and added

00:14:30.150 --> 00:14:33.480
to the open source base stack.

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:35.820
There was a lot of
performance optimizations

00:14:35.820 --> 00:14:39.180
in various components
all over the code base.

00:14:39.180 --> 00:14:41.940
Plus, we added new
APIs, which were

00:14:41.940 --> 00:14:44.820
designed from
ground up to support

00:14:44.820 --> 00:14:48.240
large scale and big clusters.

00:14:48.240 --> 00:14:52.440
We collaborated very
deeply with communities

00:14:52.440 --> 00:14:55.770
that manage products on
which Kubernetes depends

00:14:55.770 --> 00:14:58.170
on, primarily, here,
etcd and Golang,

00:14:58.170 --> 00:15:01.590
to improve the performance
that they offer to Kubernetes.

00:15:01.590 --> 00:15:05.190
And we made huge investments
into the integration

00:15:05.190 --> 00:15:07.170
layers between
Kubernetes clusters

00:15:07.170 --> 00:15:09.190
and GCP's infrastructure.

00:15:09.190 --> 00:15:12.150
So a lot of work went into
improved logging and monitoring

00:15:12.150 --> 00:15:14.760
pipelines, [INAUDIBLE] scaling
capabilities, networking

00:15:14.760 --> 00:15:17.520
components, our control
plane, its ability

00:15:17.520 --> 00:15:21.360
to configure and create clusters
and manage data configuration

00:15:21.360 --> 00:15:24.700
as they grow in scale.

00:15:24.700 --> 00:15:27.690
With all of these improvements
implemented in our tests

00:15:27.690 --> 00:15:35.670
to diagnose how it goes, as
we realized that we built

00:15:35.670 --> 00:15:37.930
a product that we
believe meets, already,

00:15:37.930 --> 00:15:40.890
the needs of our customers,
we went back to Twitter

00:15:40.890 --> 00:15:43.680
to get their feedback, whether,
indeed, the high performance

00:15:43.680 --> 00:15:46.590
build object of GKE that we
created meets their needs

00:15:46.590 --> 00:15:48.690
and will be usable for them.

00:15:52.070 --> 00:15:53.890
REZA MOTAMEDI: So
as we heard, Google

00:15:53.890 --> 00:15:57.250
has invested in rigorous
scale testing of Kubernetes,

00:15:57.250 --> 00:15:59.920
in general, and,
specifically, GKE.

00:15:59.920 --> 00:16:01.810
But Twitter was
very much interested

00:16:01.810 --> 00:16:06.050
in running its own scale test,
mainly for a couple of reasons.

00:16:06.050 --> 00:16:08.570
First, as Maciek
previously mentioned,

00:16:08.570 --> 00:16:11.080
each workload profile is unique.

00:16:11.080 --> 00:16:13.250
Specifically, for
Twitter workloads,

00:16:13.250 --> 00:16:15.610
we were interested
in scaling nodes,

00:16:15.610 --> 00:16:19.540
and Pods, and namespaces in a
number of secret dimensions.

00:16:19.540 --> 00:16:22.000
But we were not
interested in using

00:16:22.000 --> 00:16:24.850
some other resources, like
services and config maps,

00:16:24.850 --> 00:16:26.560
at least for now.

00:16:26.560 --> 00:16:29.080
This might have not been
a scenario that Google

00:16:29.080 --> 00:16:31.660
had scale tested for.

00:16:31.660 --> 00:16:35.200
Second, we wanted the
services running on GKE

00:16:35.200 --> 00:16:37.450
to work seamlessly
with services running

00:16:37.450 --> 00:16:41.650
on our current compute
setup on-premise.

00:16:41.650 --> 00:16:44.620
So we decided to use
pluggability and extensibility

00:16:44.620 --> 00:16:47.290
features in Kubernetes
and, somewhat heavily,

00:16:47.290 --> 00:16:49.300
customize the control plane.

00:16:49.300 --> 00:16:50.980
We wanted to run
our own scale tests

00:16:50.980 --> 00:16:53.650
to make sure that our
customization will also

00:16:53.650 --> 00:16:56.760
stay stable at scale.

00:16:56.760 --> 00:17:00.300
To come up with a test plan,
we followed our migration

00:17:00.300 --> 00:17:01.230
strategy.

00:17:01.230 --> 00:17:03.930
We decided to create
workload that would closely

00:17:03.930 --> 00:17:08.099
resemble what Twitter will
eventually run in Kubernetes

00:17:08.099 --> 00:17:10.589
on-prem and also in GKE.

00:17:10.589 --> 00:17:13.079
We started with what we
are currently running

00:17:13.079 --> 00:17:15.030
on Aurora Mesos clusters.

00:17:15.030 --> 00:17:18.810
But these clusters are at
least three times larger

00:17:18.810 --> 00:17:22.710
than the theoretical maximum
Kubernetes cluster sizes.

00:17:22.710 --> 00:17:26.099
So we split the
services and batch

00:17:26.099 --> 00:17:30.540
jobs running on a Mesos cluster
across three GKE clusters.

00:17:30.540 --> 00:17:33.510
As we mentioned before,
adopting Kubernetes

00:17:33.510 --> 00:17:36.510
gave us an opportunity to
run other type of application

00:17:36.510 --> 00:17:38.380
on compute as well.

00:17:38.380 --> 00:17:41.070
For instance,
stateful applications

00:17:41.070 --> 00:17:43.830
that are currently running
as bare metal services

00:17:43.830 --> 00:17:46.560
can onboard Kubernetes
as StatefulSets.

00:17:46.560 --> 00:17:50.850
And host-level services used
for host-level upgrades, content

00:17:50.850 --> 00:17:55.810
distribution, and auditing can
be deployed as daemon sets.

00:17:55.810 --> 00:17:58.270
In short, we profiled
what's running

00:17:58.270 --> 00:18:00.070
on our current
large data centers.

00:18:00.070 --> 00:18:03.990
And we spread them across
smaller Kubernetes clusters.

00:18:03.990 --> 00:18:07.140
We wanted to make the
migration to Kubernetes

00:18:07.140 --> 00:18:09.670
and cloud seamless
to our engineers.

00:18:09.670 --> 00:18:12.120
So we had to customize
Kubernetes' control plane

00:18:12.120 --> 00:18:16.800
to make GKE integrate with
our on-premise infrastructure.

00:18:16.800 --> 00:18:19.650
We created a set of custom
controllers and admission

00:18:19.650 --> 00:18:20.920
webhooks for this.

00:18:20.920 --> 00:18:24.390
And I'm going to go over a
subset of the customization

00:18:24.390 --> 00:18:26.160
that we did here.

00:18:26.160 --> 00:18:28.480
We created a
TwitterSet controller.

00:18:28.480 --> 00:18:32.340
A TwitterSet is a hybrid between
a StatefulSet and a deployment,

00:18:32.340 --> 00:18:34.410
in Kubernetes terminology.

00:18:34.410 --> 00:18:37.740
And we expect all
Twitter microservices

00:18:37.740 --> 00:18:40.370
to run as a TwitterSet.

00:18:40.370 --> 00:18:43.910
It provides a stable
numerical instance identifier,

00:18:43.910 --> 00:18:45.500
similar to StatefulSet.

00:18:45.500 --> 00:18:48.650
But it allows us to do batch
updates, similar to how

00:18:48.650 --> 00:18:50.360
deployments work.

00:18:50.360 --> 00:18:53.510
Each TwitterSet can have
multiple configuration

00:18:53.510 --> 00:18:56.120
per workload, which is
mostly used at Twitter

00:18:56.120 --> 00:18:59.250
for canarying changes.

00:18:59.250 --> 00:19:01.510
And it also provides
us with rollback

00:19:01.510 --> 00:19:04.850
on update failure, which is
similar to how Aurora Scheduler

00:19:04.850 --> 00:19:08.040
performs its updates today.

00:19:08.040 --> 00:19:10.920
We also have a few mutating
and validating webhooks,

00:19:10.920 --> 00:19:15.960
mostly to enforce consisting
naming and labeling standards,

00:19:15.960 --> 00:19:18.000
and also to enforce
security policies.

00:19:18.000 --> 00:19:20.730
For instance, we don't want
to allow foreign Docker images

00:19:20.730 --> 00:19:22.810
in our clusters.

00:19:22.810 --> 00:19:24.600
We also have a Node
Ready controller,

00:19:24.600 --> 00:19:29.520
whose job is basically to
taint nodes that do not run all

00:19:29.520 --> 00:19:32.580
the expected daemons on them.

00:19:32.580 --> 00:19:35.400
After an analysis on the
current on-premise workload,

00:19:35.400 --> 00:19:38.160
we arrived at the
following test targets.

00:19:38.160 --> 00:19:42.600
We targeted 15,000
nodes, 600 namespaces.

00:19:42.600 --> 00:19:46.620
We added 60,000 secrets that
are not evenly distributed

00:19:46.620 --> 00:19:48.210
among all the namespaces.

00:19:48.210 --> 00:19:53.250
We estimated that we are going
to run three DaemonSets and 10

00:19:53.250 --> 00:19:56.790
StatefulSets, with a
total of 8,000 Pods.

00:19:56.790 --> 00:19:59.790
We added around 400 cron jobs.

00:19:59.790 --> 00:20:03.540
And this added
another 4,000 Pods.

00:20:03.540 --> 00:20:07.470
We finally added
4,000 TwitterSets,

00:20:07.470 --> 00:20:12.080
which added another 80,000
Pods to the entire workload.

00:20:12.080 --> 00:20:14.760
So what does this
scale test look like?

00:20:14.760 --> 00:20:17.120
To minimize the
inter-test interference,

00:20:17.120 --> 00:20:20.870
each test run starts
from a blank slate

00:20:20.870 --> 00:20:22.460
and goes through
the following step.

00:20:22.460 --> 00:20:24.260
We created a new cluster.

00:20:24.260 --> 00:20:27.080
We applied Twitter
customizations.

00:20:27.080 --> 00:20:28.430
We set up some monitoring.

00:20:28.430 --> 00:20:29.600
We applied the workload.

00:20:29.600 --> 00:20:32.810
And finally we
collected results.

00:20:32.810 --> 00:20:35.960
We measured these results
in terms of service level

00:20:35.960 --> 00:20:38.030
objectives, or some SLOs.

00:20:38.030 --> 00:20:40.880
We carried some of these SLOs
from our current compute setup,

00:20:40.880 --> 00:20:44.730
since our customers are
pretty much used to them.

00:20:44.730 --> 00:20:46.760
Although in practice,
these SLOs highly

00:20:46.760 --> 00:20:48.710
depend on workload
configuration,

00:20:48.710 --> 00:20:51.740
they give us a good
baseline for comparison.

00:20:51.740 --> 00:20:53.750
I focus, here, on
two SLOs, here,

00:20:53.750 --> 00:20:57.080
that are demonstrated
in this diagram.

00:20:57.080 --> 00:20:59.930
Time to Update for TwitterSet,
in short, we call it TTU.

00:20:59.930 --> 00:21:05.780
And Time to Running for a Pod,
that is called TTR, in short.

00:21:05.780 --> 00:21:10.070
As the name suggests, TTU is the
time it takes for a TwitterSet

00:21:10.070 --> 00:21:11.990
to be fully rolled out.

00:21:11.990 --> 00:21:15.140
This, obviously, depends
on the batch size

00:21:15.140 --> 00:21:18.830
in the rolling update
strategy for the TwitterSet.

00:21:18.830 --> 00:21:21.253
In our tests, we
set that to be 20%

00:21:21.253 --> 00:21:22.670
of the size of the
workload, which

00:21:22.670 --> 00:21:25.970
is a common practice
among our engineers.

00:21:25.970 --> 00:21:31.340
We target median TTU to be below
10 minutes for a TwitterSet

00:21:31.340 --> 00:21:35.050
of the size of 5,000 replicas.

00:21:35.050 --> 00:21:38.020
Time to Running for a Pod is
a time between a Pod update

00:21:38.020 --> 00:21:40.810
is requested until it
is considered running

00:21:40.810 --> 00:21:43.300
after its update is finished.

00:21:43.300 --> 00:21:46.420
As the diagram shows, this
also includes some overhead

00:21:46.420 --> 00:21:49.150
for deleting the
old Pod, in addition

00:21:49.150 --> 00:21:52.450
to a minimum ready seconds,
which is a setting that you

00:21:52.450 --> 00:21:54.830
can set in your Pod spec.

00:21:54.830 --> 00:21:57.940
And we set that
to be 10 seconds.

00:21:57.940 --> 00:22:04.250
And we targeted this SLO to have
a median of below 40 seconds.

00:22:04.250 --> 00:22:07.240
So with that, let's
look at some results.

00:22:07.240 --> 00:22:09.730
We have the Time
to Running for Pod

00:22:09.730 --> 00:22:13.720
shown as a histogram on the
left, and Time to Update

00:22:13.720 --> 00:22:15.910
for different sizes
of TwitterSets

00:22:15.910 --> 00:22:19.510
across different measurements
as a scatter plot on the right.

00:22:19.510 --> 00:22:24.370
We see that 95% of the Pods
are transitioning to running

00:22:24.370 --> 00:22:25.965
in less than two minutes.

00:22:25.965 --> 00:22:27.340
Note, that this
actually includes

00:22:27.340 --> 00:22:31.070
that 40 second baseline
overhead, 30 seconds for Pod

00:22:31.070 --> 00:22:32.650
delete grace period,
and 10 seconds

00:22:32.650 --> 00:22:37.150
for minimum ready seconds
in our test setup.

00:22:37.150 --> 00:22:40.390
Given the baseline, we see
that mean time to running

00:22:40.390 --> 00:22:43.780
is 29 seconds, which
is within bounds.

00:22:43.780 --> 00:22:47.290
We also see that the largest
TwitterSet, with 5,000

00:22:47.290 --> 00:22:50.450
replicas, takes, on average,
seven minutes to update,

00:22:50.450 --> 00:22:55.240
which is, again, within
our 10 minutes SLO bound.

00:22:55.240 --> 00:22:58.200
So what did we learn
from all of these tests?

00:22:58.200 --> 00:23:01.860
In many cases, we benefited from
sharing our requirements early

00:23:01.860 --> 00:23:03.000
on with Google.

00:23:03.000 --> 00:23:07.160
This also helped Google with
prioritizing future work.

00:23:07.160 --> 00:23:11.070
Another point we
learned was that CRDs

00:23:11.070 --> 00:23:14.610
are stored as json, and
not protobuf struts.

00:23:14.610 --> 00:23:18.510
This is not very efficient when
you're storing large objects.

00:23:18.510 --> 00:23:21.060
But there are a few tricks
that you can use here.

00:23:21.060 --> 00:23:24.330
You can break up a large CRD,
if it contains a large array,

00:23:24.330 --> 00:23:25.420
into slices.

00:23:25.420 --> 00:23:30.720
This is similar to how endpoint
slices work in release version

00:23:30.720 --> 00:23:32.250
117.

00:23:32.250 --> 00:23:35.490
We also identified some
optimization points

00:23:35.490 --> 00:23:37.470
by just tuning
some configuration,

00:23:37.470 --> 00:23:39.120
and no code changes.

00:23:39.120 --> 00:23:41.250
For instance, we
optimized the client-go

00:23:41.250 --> 00:23:44.880
that we use inside our
TwitterSet controller

00:23:44.880 --> 00:23:50.320
by setting the qps and burst
to improve its performance.

00:23:50.320 --> 00:23:52.470
We also reduced the load
on the webhook server

00:23:52.470 --> 00:23:57.030
that we have by scoping the
webhook configuration to only

00:23:57.030 --> 00:23:58.770
look at namespace resources.

00:23:58.770 --> 00:24:03.840
And this was done to avoid the
flood of node-level events.

00:24:03.840 --> 00:24:06.450
So as we wrap up, I'm going
to give it back to Maciek

00:24:06.450 --> 00:24:08.910
to talk about some key
recommendations for you

00:24:08.910 --> 00:24:14.072
if you are preparing to
run large scale clusters.

00:24:14.072 --> 00:24:15.280
MACIEK ROZACKI: Thanks, Reza.

00:24:15.280 --> 00:24:19.270
So let's take a look at a
couple of recommended practices

00:24:19.270 --> 00:24:23.620
when you think about planning
and running your own systems

00:24:23.620 --> 00:24:25.870
at a very large scale.

00:24:25.870 --> 00:24:29.860
As you could hear,
it's important

00:24:29.860 --> 00:24:34.660
that you test and stretch
both in the planning phase,

00:24:34.660 --> 00:24:37.000
and also in later execution,
when you're testing

00:24:37.000 --> 00:24:38.410
and building your systems.

00:24:38.410 --> 00:24:41.830
You see how you, in particular,
will stretch the envelope.

00:24:41.830 --> 00:24:47.110
You need to see how your unique
workload stretches Kubernetes

00:24:47.110 --> 00:24:50.860
scalability envelope, and
also see whether you're not

00:24:50.860 --> 00:24:53.470
adding any additional
abstraction

00:24:53.470 --> 00:24:56.680
layers or specific flavors
to the workload that

00:24:56.680 --> 00:24:58.580
would apply only to you.

00:24:58.580 --> 00:25:01.480
And so it requires additional
level of validation,

00:25:01.480 --> 00:25:05.360
whether it will
operate at large scale.

00:25:05.360 --> 00:25:09.610
You can definitely rely
on us to test a set of--

00:25:09.610 --> 00:25:11.530
to validate all of
the native components,

00:25:11.530 --> 00:25:15.860
and test a set of
representative workloads.

00:25:15.860 --> 00:25:19.300
So we really verify
that the infrastructure

00:25:19.300 --> 00:25:21.550
layer that you're
going to use is going

00:25:21.550 --> 00:25:24.610
to offer high scalability.

00:25:24.610 --> 00:25:28.582
When you think about which
aspects of your clusters

00:25:28.582 --> 00:25:30.040
and workloads you
should look into,

00:25:30.040 --> 00:25:32.380
definitely validate
how are you going

00:25:32.380 --> 00:25:34.368
to use all of the native
Kubernetes resources,

00:25:34.368 --> 00:25:36.910
whether you're not going to hit
any limits that we documented

00:25:36.910 --> 00:25:38.260
and published.

00:25:38.260 --> 00:25:40.540
See how all of
the customizations

00:25:40.540 --> 00:25:44.010
perform that you
install, especially

00:25:44.010 --> 00:25:46.300
monitor what's the load
and impact that they

00:25:46.300 --> 00:25:49.390
exert on the API server.

00:25:49.390 --> 00:25:54.100
And make sure they operate
effectively at large scale.

00:25:54.100 --> 00:25:58.160
See also how your application
itself performs at large scale,

00:25:58.160 --> 00:26:00.640
whether your Pods and
Deployments are truly

00:26:00.640 --> 00:26:02.470
horizontally scalable,
or there aren't

00:26:02.470 --> 00:26:07.330
any bottlenecks that surface
when you run at large scale.

00:26:07.330 --> 00:26:11.480
You should also remember that
your clusters, in most cases,

00:26:11.480 --> 00:26:12.850
will not exist in void.

00:26:12.850 --> 00:26:16.480
They will interact with
other products in your IT

00:26:16.480 --> 00:26:17.650
infrastructure.

00:26:17.650 --> 00:26:20.750
And they will exert significant
load on some of them.

00:26:20.750 --> 00:26:22.720
So proper configuration,
for example,

00:26:22.720 --> 00:26:26.380
of databases that your
stateless microservices might

00:26:26.380 --> 00:26:28.510
be interacting with
is very important

00:26:28.510 --> 00:26:32.470
to think about, both in planning
and, later, in execution

00:26:32.470 --> 00:26:34.540
as your systems grow.

00:26:34.540 --> 00:26:38.080
To effectively monitor the
health of your systems,

00:26:38.080 --> 00:26:40.090
you need to define
a set of SLOs that

00:26:40.090 --> 00:26:43.570
build upon the standard ones
that we measure and are closer

00:26:43.570 --> 00:26:48.970
to what your end users need and
how they perceive reliability

00:26:48.970 --> 00:26:50.500
of your system.

00:26:50.500 --> 00:26:52.360
And that will give
you a proper framework

00:26:52.360 --> 00:26:56.200
to monitor infrastructure
that you're operating,

00:26:56.200 --> 00:26:57.940
and test, and iterate
if you need to make

00:26:57.940 --> 00:27:00.280
any improvements into it.

00:27:00.280 --> 00:27:04.060
There is one area of your
system's architecture

00:27:04.060 --> 00:27:07.210
that I would like to give
you a closer look on,

00:27:07.210 --> 00:27:10.000
that we did not cover
before, which is networking.

00:27:10.000 --> 00:27:15.400
It's very important that you
look at how your whole network

00:27:15.400 --> 00:27:19.360
layers design looks like and
monitor, also, how it evolves

00:27:19.360 --> 00:27:23.740
over time as your systems grow
and change, particularly, all

00:27:23.740 --> 00:27:26.170
of the load-balancing
concepts, irrespective

00:27:26.170 --> 00:27:28.660
whether you're using
Kubernetes Native,

00:27:28.660 --> 00:27:32.020
you're using the ones that
are provided by Google Cloud,

00:27:32.020 --> 00:27:34.540
or you bring your
own load balancers.

00:27:34.540 --> 00:27:38.195
They will have a certain
scalability and performance

00:27:38.195 --> 00:27:40.070
characteristics that
you take a look at, like

00:27:40.070 --> 00:27:44.230
total number of endpoints, churn
that they support, or other.

00:27:44.230 --> 00:27:46.990
You also need to account
for the efficiency

00:27:46.990 --> 00:27:49.810
and performance of your DNS.

00:27:49.810 --> 00:27:51.910
If you operate and use
DNS at large scale,

00:27:51.910 --> 00:27:54.370
definitely use
not local caching.

00:27:54.370 --> 00:27:57.580
IP management is very
important to get right,

00:27:57.580 --> 00:27:59.240
both for small and
large clusters.

00:27:59.240 --> 00:28:01.810
But in large clusters,
that's even more impactful,

00:28:01.810 --> 00:28:06.760
as a wrong IP space
configuration can either

00:28:06.760 --> 00:28:09.640
lead to your clusters
not being able to grow

00:28:09.640 --> 00:28:13.520
beyond certain limits,
or on the other hand,

00:28:13.520 --> 00:28:17.080
possibly using
excessive amount of IPs

00:28:17.080 --> 00:28:20.050
without the necessity for that.

00:28:20.050 --> 00:28:22.600
As last recommendation,
here in this context,

00:28:22.600 --> 00:28:26.170
we always recommend that
you use regional clusters

00:28:26.170 --> 00:28:27.010
for production.

00:28:27.010 --> 00:28:28.510
And for large
scale private ones,

00:28:28.510 --> 00:28:31.090
that offers the best
reliability, availability,

00:28:31.090 --> 00:28:37.510
and the best network throughput
between the master nodes

00:28:37.510 --> 00:28:39.880
and the rest of the cluster.

00:28:39.880 --> 00:28:43.250
Whenever you had any problems
or you have any doubts,

00:28:43.250 --> 00:28:45.280
please reach out to us.

00:28:45.280 --> 00:28:47.620
Your account team
or our support teams

00:28:47.620 --> 00:28:49.398
are the first line
of communication

00:28:49.398 --> 00:28:50.440
that you can engage with.

00:28:50.440 --> 00:28:52.590
And they will involve
broader groups

00:28:52.590 --> 00:28:55.750
on Google that will be very
happy to step in and help you

00:28:55.750 --> 00:28:59.410
at any stage at which you
might be with your work

00:28:59.410 --> 00:29:01.960
at running at large scale.

00:29:01.960 --> 00:29:06.430
To wrap up, I wanted to make
a couple of final remarks.

00:29:06.430 --> 00:29:10.320
As you could hear, 15,000
nodes are coming to GKE.

00:29:10.320 --> 00:29:14.220
We will be rolling them
out on top of release 118.

00:29:14.220 --> 00:29:17.280
We'd be very happy if
you decide to use them.

00:29:17.280 --> 00:29:19.500
And we are very eager
to hear any feedback

00:29:19.500 --> 00:29:23.580
you might have about them.

00:29:23.580 --> 00:29:27.090
If you have any
questions, please

00:29:27.090 --> 00:29:28.680
feel free to use the Dory.

00:29:28.680 --> 00:29:31.070
And also, here on the slide,
you can see our Twitter,

00:29:31.070 --> 00:29:33.740
to also send us
a direct message.

00:29:33.740 --> 00:29:37.150
With that, we wanted to thank
you and wish you good luck

00:29:37.150 --> 00:29:38.755
at running at large scale.

00:29:38.755 --> 00:29:40.630
REZA MOTAMEDI: And I
also wanted to thank you

00:29:40.630 --> 00:29:43.230
for tuning to our video session.

